  ],
  "text": "The first things humans ever wrote down are not poems. They are lists of grain. You imagine writing starting with a story somebody wanted to keep. It starts with somebody who wanted to remember who owed them barley. Before any symbols there were clay tokens. One shape for a sheep, another for a jar of oil. You sealed the tokens inside a clay ball so nobody could quietly swap them. But then you could not see inside, so you pressed each token into the outside first. And once the marks on the outside were enough, the tokens inside were pointless. So the ball flattened into a tablet, and the impressions became a script. Writing was not invented to express anything. It was invented to settle an argument about a debt. Literature came thousands of years later, riding on a technology built for bookkeeping. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
